1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,628
For the last few weeks, I've been building a software factory on top of Claude Code,

2
00:00:04,628 --> 00:00:06,469
and running real requests through it.

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:10,735
In this video, I'll show you how it works.

4
00:00:10,735 --> 00:00:14,381
Where it came from, what makes it different from just using agents,

5
00:00:14,381 --> 00:00:15,578
what's under the hood…

6
00:00:15,578 --> 00:00:19,332
and one real request, from the issue, all the way to a merge request.

7
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:22,271
If you already use Claude Code, this is the next step.

8
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:23,617
So…

9
00:00:23,617 --> 00:00:25,181
you already use Claude Code.

10
00:00:25,181 --> 00:00:28,810
Now imagine you stop driving every session yourself, and instead,

11
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:31,099
you build a factory that does it for you.

12
00:00:31,099 --> 00:00:33,667
Let me show you what that actually looks like.

13
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:37,445
AI coding has evolved in three steps.

14
00:00:37,445 --> 00:00:40,357
First, on your laptop, where you prompt, Claude edits,

15
00:00:40,357 --> 00:00:42,298
and you check every change yourself.

16
00:00:42,298 --> 00:00:43,593
Then came remote agents.

17
00:00:43,593 --> 00:00:46,612
You type a prompt in the Claude app, even on your phone,

18
00:00:46,612 --> 00:00:48,446
and the work happens in the cloud.

19
00:00:48,446 --> 00:00:51,681
And alongside them, something different appeared: factories.

20
00:00:51,681 --> 00:00:54,000
A factory isn't about where the agent runs.

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,995
It's about how the work is organised.

22
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,706
Put them side by side.

23
00:00:58,706 --> 00:01:01,902
Local and remote agents differ mostly in where they run.

24
00:01:01,902 --> 00:01:05,384
A factory changes who decides what gets built, who checks it,

25
00:01:05,384 --> 00:01:06,697
and what gets recorded.

26
00:01:06,697 --> 00:01:09,779
You approve the requirements before anything is built,

27
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:13,945
a separate agent checks the work, and every check leaves evidence behind.

28
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:18,669
The definition I like best comes from Dru Knox, at Tessl.

29
00:01:18,669 --> 00:01:23,130
A software factory is any agentic system where agents write all of the code you

30
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:23,412
ship.

31
00:01:23,412 --> 00:01:25,050
Which means your job changes.

32
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:26,744
You stop building the product…

33
00:01:26,744 --> 00:01:29,568
and you start building the machine that builds it.

34
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:33,986
So how do you know if your factory is any good?

35
00:01:33,986 --> 00:01:36,576
Knox uses three measures, and the order matters.

36
00:01:36,576 --> 00:01:37,440
First, autonomy.

37
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,731
How often does a person have to step in and nudge the agents?

38
00:01:40,731 --> 00:01:41,595
Then automation.

39
00:01:41,595 --> 00:01:44,455
How much can you let run without anyone reviewing it?

40
00:01:44,455 --> 00:01:45,372
And then quality.

41
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:47,908
You earn automation, by proving autonomy first.

42
00:01:49,450 --> 00:01:50,898
And what makes autonomy safe?

43
00:01:50,898 --> 00:01:51,548
Verification.

44
00:01:51,548 --> 00:01:55,044
You can only stop reading every line if something else is checking it.

45
00:01:55,044 --> 00:01:55,593
Every time.

46
00:01:55,593 --> 00:01:57,891
And leaving a record you can actually inspect.

47
00:01:57,891 --> 00:02:01,487
Without that, honestly, a factory is just a faster way to ship mistakes.

48
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:05,326
Now, a factory runs in three loops.

49
00:02:05,326 --> 00:02:09,292
The inner loop is where agents iterate before anything goes up for review.

50
00:02:09,292 --> 00:02:10,686
It's fast, and it's cheap.

51
00:02:10,686 --> 00:02:13,258
The outer loop is the slower, thorough checking,

52
00:02:13,258 --> 00:02:15,402
the kind that replaces your review time.

53
00:02:15,402 --> 00:02:17,064
And then there's the meta loop.

54
00:02:17,064 --> 00:02:19,744
That one watches the corrections and the failures…

55
00:02:19,744 --> 00:02:23,549
and fixes the factory itself, so the same mistake doesn't happen twice.

56
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:25,739
Okay.

57
00:02:25,739 --> 00:02:28,631
Here's the factory I built, on top of Claude Code.

58
00:02:28,631 --> 00:02:29,845
It has eleven stages.

59
00:02:29,845 --> 00:02:32,968
Agents write the requirements, the plan, and the code.

60
00:02:32,968 --> 00:02:36,148
Scripts do everything that can be checked mechanically.

61
00:02:36,148 --> 00:02:38,230
And a person steps in exactly twice.

62
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:40,677
And here's the thing.

63
00:02:40,677 --> 00:02:42,547
The whole pipeline is just data.

64
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:46,403
Each stage says who does the work, an agent, a script, or a human,

65
00:02:46,403 --> 00:02:49,616
which skill it follows, and what it's allowed to write.

66
00:02:49,616 --> 00:02:53,531
If a stage fails three times, the factory stops, and asks a person.

67
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:57,584
Every kind of job has a skill.

68
00:02:57,584 --> 00:03:00,358
It's just a short, written instruction.

69
00:03:00,358 --> 00:03:02,065
This one's for UI logic.

70
00:03:02,065 --> 00:03:04,412
Pure functions, no DOM, no clock.

71
00:03:04,412 --> 00:03:06,261
And look at the last line.

72
00:03:06,261 --> 00:03:11,240
The maker never ticks its own task, and never writes its own evidence.

73
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:12,908
Alright.

74
00:03:12,908 --> 00:03:15,942
Let's watch one real request, go all the way through.

75
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:21,106
It starts as a plain GitLab issue, with the factory label on it.

76
00:03:21,106 --> 00:03:23,506
A webhook kicks off a pass, straight away.

77
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:28,424
Within about a minute, the requirements are written.

78
00:03:28,424 --> 00:03:29,968
And then the factory stops…

79
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:31,283
and waits for a person.

80
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:35,373
The requirements are user stories, with acceptance criteria.

81
00:03:35,373 --> 00:03:37,760
Everything downstream gets checked against these,

82
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:39,806
so this approval actually means something.

83
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:43,157
Approving is one word on the issue.

84
00:03:43,157 --> 00:03:43,986
Oh, and no slash.

85
00:03:43,986 --> 00:03:46,911
GitLab quietly swallows a comment that's only slash approve.

86
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:49,713
Next, comes the plan.

87
00:03:49,713 --> 00:03:53,802
The work is split into sections, and every section ends with a gate,

88
00:03:53,802 --> 00:03:55,546
that the checker has to pass.

89
00:03:57,450 --> 00:03:59,594
And these are the checker's gates.

90
00:03:59,594 --> 00:04:02,937
They're written into the plan before any code exists.

91
00:04:02,937 --> 00:04:05,271
Real commands, with expected results.

92
00:04:05,271 --> 00:04:09,118
The module's exports, no DOM access, the currency formatting,

93
00:04:09,118 --> 00:04:11,010
totals right down to the cent.

94
00:04:12,450 --> 00:04:14,368
This is the inner loop in action.

95
00:04:14,368 --> 00:04:16,286
A maker session builds a section.

96
00:04:16,286 --> 00:04:20,820
Then a completely separate checker session, one that never saw the maker work,

97
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:21,634
runs the gate.

98
00:04:21,634 --> 00:04:23,842
If it passes, the next section starts.

99
00:04:23,842 --> 00:04:24,540
If it fails…

100
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:26,865
a fresh maker gets the written findings.

101
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:30,578
The checker writes down its evidence.

102
00:04:30,578 --> 00:04:33,913
What it ran, what it expected, and what it actually found.

103
00:04:33,913 --> 00:04:34,603
In this run…

104
00:04:34,603 --> 00:04:36,846
four sections, four passes, first time.

105
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:40,220
Then, the outer loop kicks in.

106
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:44,880
A script re-runs the checks instead of trusting them, the full test suite runs,

107
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,178
and a preview goes up.

108
00:04:47,450 --> 00:04:52,500
Now, the person who asked for it gets to try the real thing, right in a browser.

109
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,573
And they approve…

110
00:04:53,573 --> 00:04:53,952
again.

111
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:59,537
And then a merge request opens, with the specs and every bit of evidence linked.

112
00:04:59,537 --> 00:05:01,171
Merging is still a human's call.

113
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:04,231
So how does it actually run?

114
00:05:04,231 --> 00:05:07,283
An issue or a comment in GitLab fires a webhook,

115
00:05:07,283 --> 00:05:09,763
and that starts a pipeline on a runner.

116
00:05:09,763 --> 00:05:14,342
Each pass pulls the factory's state from a git branch, walks the stages,

117
00:05:14,342 --> 00:05:17,140
and runs claude dash p for every agent step.

118
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:21,846
Then it pushes the work, comments on the issue, and saves its state again.

119
00:05:21,846 --> 00:05:23,627
Nothing runs on your laptop.

120
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:26,918
Mapped back to the loops…

121
00:05:26,918 --> 00:05:31,029
the inner loop is maker and checker, per section, with three attempts.

122
00:05:31,029 --> 00:05:34,787
The outer loop is scripts and tests, and then a person tries it.

123
00:05:34,787 --> 00:05:35,844
And the meta loop?

124
00:05:35,844 --> 00:05:38,722
Every time someone has to step in, it's recorded,

125
00:05:38,722 --> 00:05:41,071
and the fix goes into a skill or a rule.

126
00:05:41,071 --> 00:05:41,599
Honestly…

127
00:05:41,599 --> 00:05:43,185
right now, that loop is me.

128
00:05:43,185 --> 00:05:43,654
By hand.

129
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:47,288
Here are the numbers from that run.

130
00:05:47,288 --> 00:05:50,753
Seventeen minutes from issue to merge request, approvals included.

131
00:05:50,753 --> 00:05:51,751
Ten agent sessions.

132
00:05:51,751 --> 00:05:54,166
About a dollar and twenty cents at API prices.

133
00:05:54,166 --> 00:05:57,264
And every single section passed its checker the first time.

134
00:05:58,450 --> 00:05:59,491
Now, the limits.

135
00:05:59,491 --> 00:06:01,378
Checks prove the checks pass…

136
00:06:01,378 --> 00:06:03,199
not that the spec was right.

137
00:06:03,199 --> 00:06:06,192
That's exactly why a person approves the spec.

138
00:06:06,192 --> 00:06:08,143
The meta loop is still manual.

139
00:06:08,143 --> 00:06:10,811
The runner handles one request at a time.

140
00:06:10,811 --> 00:06:13,608
And it works best on small, clear requests.

141
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:16,814
Want to try it yourself?

142
00:06:16,814 --> 00:06:18,008
There's a playground.

143
00:06:18,008 --> 00:06:20,794
Your own GitLab project, with nothing to install.

144
00:06:20,794 --> 00:06:23,012
File an issue, and watch it go through.

145
00:06:23,012 --> 00:06:26,707
The code is open source, and there's a longer write up on Medium.
